{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.5,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["U3", "U7"],
      "pins": ["U3.9", "U3.20", "U7.6"],
      "nets": ["NET0009"],
      "observed": "Keyboard column COL2 (U3 pin 9) and keyboard row ROW4 (U3 pin 20) both carry the net label NET0009. The same label also appears on the SKW92A top-edge pin label group on page 2. Row and column lines of the BBQ10KBD matrix therefore resolve to one node.",
      "expected": "Each matrix row and column should be a separate net, joined only through the key switches. The spec describes 5 column lines and 7 row lines wired directly to separate SKW92A GPIO pins, so a column and a row should never share a net.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "COL2 9 ... NET0009 (page 2, keyboard U3 block)"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "ROW4 20 ... NET0009 (page 2, keyboard U3 block)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "BBQ10KBD ... 5 column + 7 row lines wired directly to SKW92A GPIO pins (matrix scanned by the module itself, not via an onboard keyboard controller)"}
      ],
      "impact": "Pressing keys would create shorts between a column and a row line. Matrix scanning would misread keys, and the GPIO drivers could fight each other.",
      "confidence": 0.5
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The power section, EPD driver values and barrel-jack wiring match the spec. The one concrete deviation I can read off the drawing is that keyboard column COL2 and row ROW4 share the same net label, NET0009, which would short a matrix row to a column; confidence is moderate because the label reading comes from a dense tile."
}

